## 10. Best Practices Used

### 10.1 Separation of Concerns

| Layer | Responsibility | Files |
|---|---|---|
| Auth | Token acquisition & HTTP client | `auth/` |
| Endpoints | URL construction | `endpoints/` |
| Queries | SQL logic | `queries/` |
| Utilities | Business comparison logic | `utils/` |
| Tests | Orchestration + assertions | `tests/` |

No test file contains SQL directly. No utility function contains assertions.

### 10.2 Singleton DB Client

`dbClient` is a module-level singleton. All tests in a `describe` block share one connection pool (opened in `beforeAll`, closed in `afterAll`), avoiding connection exhaustion.

### 10.3 Global Setup for Authentication

Authentication runs **exactly once** per test suite execution (`globalSetup`). Token is persisted to disk so any test can access it, even after process restarts.

### 10.4 Parameterized SQL (No Hardcoding)

All SQL files use `{USER_ID}`, `{FAMILY_ID}`, `{END_DATE}` placeholders. No user IDs or dates are hardcoded in SQL files. Values are injected at runtime from environment variables.

### 10.5 Threshold Configuration via Environment

The comparison threshold (`COMPARISON_THRESHOLD_PCT=0.25`) is environment-driven. Any test can override by passing a `threshold` parameter, but the default comes from `.env`. This means threshold adjustments don't require code changes.

### 10.6 Mirrored Test Structure (Individual / Family / Manage-Assets)

The three test scopes (`tests/dashboard/`, `tests/family/dashboard/`, `tests/manage-assets/dashboard/`) are kept structurally identical. Same test names, same SQL patterns — only the user scope and SQL file path differ.

### 10.7 Rich Test Attachments

Every test attaches both human-readable (`.txt`) and machine-readable (`.json`) comparison data to the Playwright HTML report. This eliminates the need to re-run tests to debug failures.

### 10.8 Defensive SQL with COALESCE

All SQL queries use `COALESCE(..., 0)` for every asset class sum. This ensures NULL values from missing accounts don't propagate and corrupt the grand total.

